<title>USA: UN sanctions head meets Aziz, no details on sales.</title>
<headline>UN sanctions head meets Aziz, no details on sales.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-06-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>The head of the U.N. sanctions committee met on Monday with Iraqi Deputy Prime Minister Tareq Aziz to discuss the resumption of the &quot;oil-for-food&quot; accord.</p>
<p>Portuguese Ambassador and U.N. sanctions head Antonio Monteiro said he hoped the process to restart oil sales, following Iraq's submission of a new aid distribution plan, would begin soon.</p>
<p>But Monteiro said he could offer no fresh details after his meeting with Aziz on when the aid plan would be approved and oil sales restarted.</p>
<p>&quot;We discussed the main issues that are at stake, and I hope that we'll be able from here to have a better understanding and work together better,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>On Monday, Iraqi diplomat and lead negotiator for the oil accord, Abdul Amir al-Anbari, reiterated his belief that oil would flow again in early August.</p>
<p>Iraq is allowed to sell $2 billion worth of crude for a 180-day period, mainly to buy food and medicine for civilians suffering under trade sanctions.</p>
<p>((--Patrick Connole NY Energy Desk ((+1 212 859 1620))</p>
